WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.477
Approximate nearest neighbor search, and the metric that judges it.

2
00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:12.312
Approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) indexes return vectors that are probably among the true nearest neighbors, much faster than checking them all.

3
00:00:12.592 --> 00:00:17.051
They don’t promise the exact answer, so you need a number that says how close they get.

4
00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:27.955
That number is recall@k against exact search: for a query, take the exact top k and the index’s top k, count how many vectors appear in both, and divide by k.

5
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:30.112
Average over a sample of queries.

6
00:00:30.392 --> 00:00:32.653
An index with recall@10 of 0.

7
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:35.472
95 returns, on average, 9.

8
00:00:35.752 --> 00:00:37.864
5 of the true 10 nearest neighbors.

9
00:00:38.144 --> 00:00:40.619
Keep this separate from the recall in lesson 2.

10
00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:46.936
That one compared retrieved documents with human relevance labels: did the answer make it into the context?

11
00:00:47.216 --> 00:00:53.616
This one compares the index with exact search on the same vectors: did the index find what brute force would have?

12
00:00:53.896 --> 00:01:07.187
The total error in your system is a product of both: an index can only lose relevant documents that the embedding model ranked highly, and a perfect index can’t rescue a model that ranks the right document 200th.

13
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:11.584
When retrieval quality drops, measuring both tells you which part to fix.
